  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION FOR THE 00Z TAF PACKAGE...\n\nHigh pressure continues to weaken over the region today with \nstronger onshore flow seen at current observations. This has allowed \nfor a deepening marine layer, where some places along the coast saw \nlow clouds and patchy fog earlier this morning. Looking toward the \nlunch hour, temperatures a relatively cooler with highs in the 70s \nto near 90 west of the mountains. Elevated westerly winds will also \nbe felt across the mountains and deserts with wind gusts 20-40 MPH. \n\nAn area of low pressure off the west coast of British Columbia will \ncontinue to push southward today, eventually moving into the Pacific \nNorthwest this weekend. This weather pattern will help bring in more \ncooler air and greater extent to the marine layer into far western \nvalleys. We can expect to cool a few degrees each day with breezy \nwesterly winds across the mountains and deserts each afternoon and \nevening. Temperatures will be near average by the weekend, with \nhighs dipping below average by next Monday and Tuesday.  High \ntemperatures may stay below 90 degrees for areas west of the \nmountains with highs closer to 100 degrees over the Coachella \nValley. Low temperatures will also begin to cool off through early \nnext week as well with lows in the 50s and 60s along and west of the \nmountains and up in the high desert.\n\nHigh pressure over the Southern Plains may expand further into the \ndesert southwest by the end of next week. The placement and strength \nof this system is still uncertain, but many models are hinting that \nanother subtle warm up may take place by this time. NBM shows highs \napproaching 100 across inland valleys with 70s and 80s closer to the \ncoast and across the mountains.",
